{"uid":"e1b57d54737e92be","name":"test_a_cure_diff_that_misses_the_cited_path_un_reopens_the_record","fullName":"tests.test_cure_invalidates#test_a_cure_diff_that_misses_the_cited_path_un_reopens_the_record","historyId":"4ac6eced266bf8a87908d4ff5b331099","time":{"start":1791976718468,"stop":1791976718841,"duration":373},"description":"Cure-01-05 (PRD requirement B.3/B.4, F05, #221) rewrites this node IN\n    PLACE (zero fast-tier headroom -- one collected item, same as before\n    this task) to loop over ALL SIX prior terminal statuses a record could\n    genuinely have held before the reopen -- `completed` (this test's own\n    original subject), `validated`, `failed`, `skipped`, `superseded` and\n    (task 001, F04) `petitioned` -- instead of adding five siblings.\n    `validated` is in the list for this NON-VIGILANT run deliberately: it\n    is F05's own probe, and the reason the un-reopen edge is declared for\n    both variants rather than split across the vigilant delta. A record's\n    status is data on disk, so the flag in force at reconciliation time is\n    no evidence about what it held at reopen time; before this task the\n    restore asked a non-vigilant table for `pending -> validated`, was\n    refused, and left the record `pending` with no `task.un_reopened`\n    event and no notification, while a `failed`/`skipped`/`superseded`\n    record was silently PROMOTED to `completed`. Each iteration also\n    asserts the persisted provenance the production hook reads instead of\n    guessing -- `preCureStatuses` and the reopen event's own `priorStatus`\n    -- and the notification's own text, which now names the status each\n    record was restored TO; the tail of the node covers the one shape that\n    carries no persisted prior status at all, where the hook leaves the\n    record `pending` rather than inventing one.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>Cure-01-05 (PRD requirement B.3/B.4, F05, #221) rewrites this node IN<br />\nPLACE (zero fast-tier headroom -- one collected item, same as before<br />\nthis task) to loop over ALL SIX prior terminal statuses a record could<br />\ngenuinely have held before the reopen -- <code>completed</code> (this test's own<br />\noriginal subject), <code>validated</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>skipped</code>, <code>superseded</code> and<br />\n(task 001, F04) <code>petitioned</code> -- instead of adding five siblings.<br />\n<code>validated</code> is in the list for this NON-VIGILANT run deliberately: it<br />\nis F05's own probe, and the reason the un-reopen edge is declared for<br />\nboth variants rather than split across the vigilant delta. A record's<br />\nstatus is data on disk, so the flag in force at reconciliation time is<br />\nno evidence about what it held at reopen time; before this task the<br />\nrestore asked a non-vigilant table for <code>pending -&gt; validated</code>, was<br />\nrefused, and left the record <code>pending</code> with no <code>task.un_reopened</code><br />\nevent and no notification, while a <code>failed</code>/<code>skipped</code>/<code>superseded</code><br />\nrecord was silently PROMOTED to <code>completed</code>. Each iteration also<br />\nasserts the persisted provenance the production hook reads instead of<br />\nguessing -- <code>preCureStatuses</code> and the reopen event's own <code>priorStatus</code><br />\n-- and the notification's own text, which now names the status each<br />\nrecord was restored TO; the tail of the node covers the one shape that<br />\ncarries no persisted prior status at all, where the hook leaves the<br />\nrecord <code>pending</code> rather than inventing one.</p>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791976554800,"stop":1791976554801,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmen